# Project: Study Focus Companion (Chrome Extension)

## What it is
A Chrome extension that helps students stay focused while studying. The user sets a
goal (task name + duration), then studies in other tabs. A friendly character appears
on whatever website they are on to gently nudge them when they get distracted, and
suggests short breaks when they keep reaching for their phone. It also tracks whether
they are on pace to finish their task in time.

Built for a Women in CS hackathon, "Off the Clock (Everyday Life)" track. Priority is a
reliable, polished live demo over extra features.

## Core features
1. Goal setup: task name + study duration.
2. Session: timer, pause/end, pace indicator ("on pace" / "may need more time").
3. Phone detection: webcam + TensorFlow.js COCO-SSD detects when the user picks up
   their phone. Repeated pickups trigger a break suggestion.
4. Distracting-site detection: if the active tab is on a distracting site during a
   session, the character gives a gentle nudge.
5. Character overlay: appears in the bottom-right of any website with a speech bubble
   and buttons (e.g. "Take a break" / "I'm good").
6. Session summary: focused time, pickups, distractions, breaks taken.
7. (Stretch) Character messages generated by Grok from text-only session stats.

## Architecture (Manifest V3, React + Vite, WXT preferred)
- Session page (`session.html`, opened in its own tab): goal screen, timer, webcam,
  phone detection, pickup logic, pace tracking, summary. This is the "brain" and keeps
  running in a background tab while the user studies elsewhere.
- Background service worker: relays messages between the session page and content
  scripts. Watches active tab changes/URLs for distracting sites. Cannot use the camera.
- Content script: injected on all websites. Renders the character overlay inside a
  Shadow DOM so site CSS cannot affect it. Listens for messages to show the character.
- Shared messages file: the single source of truth for all message types and
  send/listen helpers. Every part of the extension must use it.

Clicking the extension icon opens the session page in a new tab. Do NOT run the camera
or detection in the popup, background worker, or content script.

## Message types (defined in the shared messages file)
- SESSION_START { task, durationMinutes, startTime }
- SESSION_END { summary }
- PICKUP_DETECTED { timestamp }
- SUGGEST_BREAK { pickupCount, windowMinutes }
- DISTRACTING_SITE { url }
- SHOW_CHARACTER { state, text, buttons? }
- CHARACTER_RESPONSE { action }  // e.g. "take_break", "dismiss"

Character states: idle, nudge, breakSuggestion, celebrate.
Do not add or rename message types without updating the shared file and telling the team.

## Phone detection rules
- Use @tensorflow-models/coco-ssd with TensorFlow.js, installed as npm packages and
  bundled in the extension. Never load scripts or models from a CDN (MV3 blocks it).
- Run detection about once per second, not every frame.
- Only count "cell phone" detections with confidence above 0.6.
- A pickup counts when a phone is detected in 3 consecutive checks after being absent
  for at least 10 seconds.
- Send SUGGEST_BREAK when there are 3+ pickups within the last 10 minutes.
- Keep all thresholds as named, configurable constants at the top of the file.
- Include an optional debug overlay that draws bounding boxes on the video preview.

## Privacy rules (non-negotiable)
- Never record, save, or upload video or images. Frames are analyzed in memory and
  discarded.
- Never send webcam frames to any server or AI API, including Grok. Grok only ever
  receives text stats (e.g. "3 pickups in 10 minutes, 40 of 90 minutes done").
- Store only events and counts (timestamps, focused minutes, breaks) in local storage.
- Stop all camera media tracks when a session is paused or ended so the camera light
  turns off.
- Show a consent screen explaining what is analyzed before enabling the camera.
- Provide a visible camera-on indicator, a pause button, and a screen-only mode that
  works without the camera (including if permission is denied).

## Character tone
Warm, encouraging, a little playful. Never scolding, guilt-tripping, or sarcastic.
Break suggestions should point toward restorative breaks (stretch, water, short walk),
not scrolling. Keep messages short (one or two sentences).

## Code conventions
- TypeScript, React function components, small focused files.
- Keep each person's work inside their own folder (see ownership below).
- Prefer simple, readable code over clever abstractions; this is a hackathon.
- Handle errors gracefully (camera denied, model fails to load) without crashing.
- Do not add a backend server unless explicitly asked.

## Team ownership
- Person 1, Detection: session page webcam, COCO-SSD detection, pickup logic,
  calibration, camera controls.
- Person 2, Character: content script overlay, Shadow DOM, character states and
  animations, speech bubbles, buttons, backup system notifications.
- Person 3, Session flow + messaging: goal screen, timer, pace tracking, summary,
  background worker, distracting-site detection, local storage of stats.
- Person 4, Content + polish + pitch: character messages and personality, consent and
  privacy screen, Grok message generation (text only), testing, demo prep.

Changes to the shared messages file or this rules file should be agreed on by the team.

## Demo notes
- Do not demo on chrome:// pages, the new tab page, or the Chrome Web Store; content
  scripts cannot run there.
- Load the extension via chrome://extensions > Developer mode > Load unpacked.
- Test detection in the actual venue lighting before judging.
